# =============================================================================
# Line endings
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# The repository's index was already 100% LF, but with `core.autocrlf=true`
# Git converted LF to CRLF on checkout. That produced 48 fully-CRLF working-tree
# files and 26 more with *mixed* endings inside a single file. The visible
# symptom was phantom `git diff` output: status reported a file as modified, a
# diff printed hunks, and a byte-level comparison showed the content identical
# to HEAD. Two people editing at once saw three different "diffs" for one file
# within a minute, which reads as lost work and is not.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine Git setting, so relying on it means every
# developer and every CI runner has to be configured identically or the
# worktree diverges from the index. Checking the rule into the repository makes
# the contract explicit and machine-independent instead.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect binary content itself and skip normalisation for
# it, so the explicit binary rules below are belt-and-braces documentation
# rather than load-bearing exclusions.
# =============================================================================

# Default: normalise to LF in the index and check out LF on every platform.
# The Docker builds, the CI runners and the Linux deploy targets all assume LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows scripts
#
# These are run by PowerShell on Windows only. They are the one place CRLF is
# correct rather than merely tolerated.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary assets - never line-ending normalised.
#
# Without these, a file that happens to contain 0x0D 0x0A bytes in a font or a
# PNG could be "normalised" and corrupted. `text=auto` already detects these,
# but naming them keeps the intent obvious to anyone editing this file later.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.jpeg      binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.webp      binary
*.avif      binary
*.svg       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.ttf       binary
*.otf       binary
*.eot       binary
*.pdf       binary
*.zip       binary
*.gz        binary
*.svgz      binary

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lockfiles and generated assets
#
# Bun generates this and it is already LF. Declaring it keeps a future
# `text=auto` misdetection from rewriting it, and gives a reviewer an obvious
# place to look when a lockfile diff shows phantom whole-file changes.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
bun.lock    text eol=lf